**Founding claims that didn't survive the full texts.** The guide quoted these, so I rewrote the lines (`docs/writing-guide.md`, and Q2/Q5 in `docs/research-plan.md`):
- **P2:** the "~41% of unstated requirements guessed right" figure measures something else. The finding is that an unstated requirement loses ~22.6% accuracy (conditional requirements are met unprompted only 22.9% of the time), and stating everything also costs. The line now reads: state what the reader won't do by default, and leave out what it will.
- **P4:** "the strongest model gained most from scaffolding" holds only within Anthropic models, at GAIA Level 2, on one slice. GPT-5.5, which had the highest baseline, gained least. The guide also cited 2604.27891 as support for keeping structure, which that paper doesn't show. The line now says procedural scaffolding gets absorbed as models improve, while structure that carries knowledge, verification or enforcement keeps paying.
- **P6:** in the current version of the AGENTS.md paper, the harm from LLM-written files is not statistically significant, and the human-vs-LLM comparison isn't controlled. The cause looks like repeating documentation the agent can already find, not who wrote the file.
- **P3:** the chain-of-thought citation was overstated, but runtime enforcement and typed allowlists now support it well. For example, prompt-only policies were attacked successfully 5/5 times, against 0/5 when enforced at runtime.
- **Other bad data:** 2510.22251 ("Prompting Inversion") has integrity problems, including case-study IDs that aren't in GSM8K and a model name that never existed. I gave it credibility 1.

**Evidence tags now:** P2, P3 and P5 are `[strong]`; P1, P4 and P6 are `[moderate]`. No line is `[provisional]` any more.

